course: physics
lesson: 19
title: Bangs and loud sounds: firecrackers and ears
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A bang is one sudden, hard shove on the air: instead of a gentle wobble, the air is pushed out all at once in a single sharp wave.
explain: Inside a firecracker a small amount of powder burns in a flash and turns into a lot of hot gas all at once; the gas bursts outwards and slams the air, and that slam reaches your ears as a crack.
explain: Close up, that slam is strong enough to hurt: it hits your eardrum with a sudden hard push, and in someone's hand the hot gas can break bones and burn skin, which is why only trained adults handle fireworks.
explain: A popping balloon makes a small bang in the same way, when its squashed air escapes all at once.
explain: Ears are delicate: the tiny hair cells inside never grow back, so a ringing in your ears after a bang is a warning that they have been hurt.
fact: A firecracker bangs because the powder inside burns in a flash, making a lot of hot gas that shoves the air outwards in one sharp pressure wave.
fact: A firecracker close to your ears can tear the eardrum or kill the tiny hair cells inside the ear, and those cells never grow back.
fact: Ringing in the ears after a loud bang is a warning sign that the ears have been damaged.
fact: A firecracker going off in a hand can break bones and burn flesh, and every year hospitals treat children who lost fingers to fireworks.
fact: A firework that did not go off can still explode later, so you never go back to a lit firework; you leave it and tell an adult.
fact: Dogs and cats hide on fireworks night because their hearing is far more sensitive than ours and they do not understand the sudden bangs.
fact: People help pets on fireworks night by keeping them indoors with the curtains closed and some quiet music on.
fact: A balloon bursts at a pin's touch because the tiny hole lets the stretched rubber rip apart in an instant, and the squashed air rushes out all at once.
fact: A piece of sticky tape on a balloon can let a pin go in without a bang, because the tape stops the rubber ripping.
fact: A slammed door bangs because it hits the frame and pushes the air away suddenly; a soft rubber strip on the frame makes it quieter.
fact: Noise-cancelling headphones listen to the noise and play the opposite wave, so the push and the pull cancel out; they work best on steady droning noise (a low, steady hum, like a train or a plane engine).
fact: Sound spreads out in every direction and gets weaker with distance, so a faraway shout is faint.
fact: Sound effects copy real sounds with other things, such as coconut shells for horses' hooves, because our brains recognise the pattern of the wobbles.
exercise: Why does a firecracker make a sharp bang? | Its powder burns in a flash and the gas slams the air outwards | A tiny bell inside it is struck | It sucks all the air in at once | The paper wrapper tears with a crack
exercise: Why does a firecracker bang hurt your ears close up? | The pressure wave hits your eardrum with a sudden hard push | The bright light of the flash hurts the ears as well as the eyes | The smoke goes into your ears | The cold air from it freezes the ear
exercise: What can a very loud bang do to the tiny hair cells inside your ears? | Kill them, and they never grow back | Make them grow longer and stronger | Make them fall out and grow back next week | Nothing, they cannot be harmed
exercise: Your ears ring after a loud bang. What does that mean? | It is a warning sign that your ears were damaged | It means your ears are getting stronger and tougher | It is how ears clean themselves | It means you have caught a cold
exercise: Why can a firecracker going off in a hand badly injure it? | The burst of hot gas pushes with enormous force right at the skin | The noise alone breaks the fingers | The paper cuts the skin like a knife | The colours burn the eyes, not the hand
exercise: Who should handle fireworks? | Only trained adults | Any child who is careful | Anyone wearing gloves | Anyone older than eight
exercise: A firework was lit but did not go off. What should you do? | Never go back to it; leave it and tell an adult | Pick it up and throw it far away | Light it again carefully so that it finishes going off | Put it in your pocket to take home
exercise: Why do dogs and cats hide on fireworks night? | Their hearing is far more sensitive, and the bangs terrify them | They do not like the bright colours, which hurt their eyes in the dark sky | They are trying to keep warm | They want to sleep early
exercise: How can people help their pets on fireworks night? | Keep them inside with curtains closed and quiet music on | Take them out to watch the fireworks so that they get used to the bangs | Leave them alone in the garden | Hold them close to the bonfire
exercise: Why does a balloon bang when a pin touches it? | The rubber rips in an instant and the squashed air rushes out at once | The pin is so sharp it makes a sound | The air inside explodes like a firework | The balloon turns into gas
exercise: How can a pin go into a balloon without a bang? | Through a piece of sticky tape that stops the rip | Only if the pin has been heated up until it is very hot | Only in very cold weather, when the rubber goes stiff | It never can, because a pin always makes a balloon bang
exercise: Why does a slammed door make a bang? | It hits the frame and pushes the air away suddenly | Doors have a spring that cracks | The handle makes the bang when it hits, not the door itself | The house shakes and the walls bang
exercise: How do noise-cancelling headphones make a train journey quiet? | They play the opposite wave, so the push and pull cancel out | They are filled with thick foam only | They play louder music over the noise | They block your ears with a magnet
exercise: Why does a person shouting from far away sound faint? | The sound spreads out and gets weaker with distance | Sound slows down as it travels and then stops after a while | The air eats up most of the shout before it can reach you | Your ears are only able to hear people who are close to you
exercise: How can coconut shells sound like horses' hooves in a radio play? | They copy the pattern of the wobbles the hooves make | They are made of the same stuff as hooves | Coconuts are recorded at a farm | The radio adds a horse sound by itself when it hears the shells
exercise: Which kind of noise do noise-cancelling headphones handle best? | Steady droning noise, like a train or a plane engine | Sudden voices and shouts from people sitting near you | Only birdsong | Only very high whistles
test: my bro says a dud firework is safe to pick up after a minute. right? | No, it can still go off later, leave it and tell an adult | Yes, after a minute it is dead | Yes, if you pour a little water on it first, it is completely dead | No, but after five minutes it is safe
test: mate held a banger in his hand for a laugh in a video. what's the real danger? | It can break bones, burn skin and take off fingers | None, bangers are only loud | It only hurts if the banger is wet or old, otherwise it is fine | It just makes your hand tingle for a bit
test: What would happen if a firecracker burst far away across a field instead of next to you? | The bang would be much weaker by the time it reached you | It would be just as loud as close up | It would be silent, because sound cannot cross fields | It would be louder, because sound grows with distance
test: In a cartoon a firecracker goes off in a cat's paws and the cat just has sooty fur. What is wrong? | Real fireworks can badly injure anyone who holds them | Nothing, that is exactly what happens | Real cats are immune to bangs | Real fireworks only ever burn your hair and never your skin
test: Why is a balloon pop like a tiny firecracker bang? | Both are squashed gas escaping all at once in one sharp wave | Both are made of the same powder | Both are lit with a match | Both bang because of the light they make
test: Bloody neighbours with their fireworks again, my dog's shaking under the bed. What helps? | Keep him in, curtains shut, some quiet music on | Take him outside so he gets used to it and calms down | Shout at him to stop shaking | Let him run loose in the garden
test: Why is ringing in the ears after a concert or a bang not something to laugh off? | It warns of damage to hair cells that never grow back | It always goes away by the morning, so it means nothing at all | It means your ears are getting tougher | It is just your brain humming the song
test: Which is true about how sound weakens? | It spreads out over a bigger area the further it goes | It gets louder and louder the further it travels from the bang | It stops completely after it has travelled one kilometre or so | It weakens only at night, when the air is cold and heavy
test: A new rubber strip is stuck on the door frame. What will change? | The door will close with a quieter thud | The door will bang even louder than before | The door will not close properly at all any more | The door will swing open on its own in a draught
test: A friend says noise-cancelling headphones work by just being very thick. What is the cleverer truth? | They play the opposite wave to cancel the noise | They are very thick and soft, and nothing more than that | They suck the sound away into a little box inside them | They turn the noise into light that you cannot see
test: A film crew crunches celery for the sound of a breaking bone. Why does it work? | Our brains recognise the pattern of the wobbles, not the object | Celery and bones are made of the same stuff | The audience cannot hear the difference between any two sounds at all | The microphone changes every sound into bones
exam: Identify the principal mechanism by which a firecracker produces its report. | The rapid conversion of its charge into hot gas, which displaces the air in a single pressure wave | The vibration of a metallic component within the casing | The rupture of the paper casing alone | The emission of intense light
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appropriate conduct in respect of a firework that has failed to ignite? | It is not to be approached, and an adult is to be informed | It may be retrieved after a brief interval of a minute or thereabouts | It is to be relit forthwith | It may safely be handled if dampened
exam: State the consequence of acoustic injury to the sensory hair cells of the inner ear. | The cells are not regenerated and the damage is permanent | The cells regenerate entirely within the space of about a week | The cells are strengthened by the exposure | No consequence ensues
exam: Ascertain the principle by which noise-cancelling headphones reduce ambient noise. | A wave opposite to the incident noise is generated so that the two cancel | The noise is absorbed by foam exclusively | The noise is masked by music of greater volume | The noise is converted into light
